   152	## 2. The residual divisor is the inverse Kummer class
   153	
   154	Choose an irreducible reduced polynomial `c_0` defining `C_0`.  Since
   155	`pi|U` is etale, its pullback of the reduced Cartier divisor `C_0` is reduced.
   156	The normalization map `Phi->C_0` has generic degree one, so `Phi` occurs with
   157	coefficient one.  There is therefore a unique residual reduced effective
   158	divisor `E`, with no component `Phi`, such that
   159	
   160	```text
   161	div_U(c_0 o pi)=Phi+E.                                  (2.1)
   162	```
   163	
   164	Taking divisor classes and using principality gives
   165	
   166	```text
   167	[E]=-[Phi].                                             (2.2)
   168	```
   169	
   170	In particular, the total class `[E]` has exact order `mu`.  This does not
   171	say that `E` is irreducible or that every component of `E` has order `mu`.
   172	
   173	In `C(U)` define
   174	
   175	```text
   176	r=(c_0 o pi)^mu/(t-a).
   177	```
   178	
   179	Equations (2.1) and `div_U(t-a)=mu*Phi` give
   180	
   181	```text
   182	div_U(r)=mu*E>=0.                                       (2.3)
   183	```
   184	
   185	The smooth affine surface `U` is normal.  Its coordinate ring is the
   186	intersection of its height-one valuation rings inside `C(U)`, so (2.3)
   187	implies `r in O(U)`.  This proves the exact identity
   188	
   189	```text
   190	(t-a)r=(c_0 o pi)^mu.                                  (2.4)
   191	```
   192	
   193	Let `alpha^mu=t-a` in the canonical cyclic field.  Then
   194	
   195	```text
   196	beta=(c_0 o pi)/alpha,        beta^mu=r.
   197	```
   198	
   199	Conversely `alpha=(c_0 o pi)/beta`.  Hence
   200	
   201	```text
   202	C(U)(alpha)=C(U)(beta).                                 (2.5)
   203	```
   204	
   205	Equivalently, in `C(U)^*/C(U)^{*mu}` the classes of `t-a` and `r` are
   206	mutual inverses.  The two effective torsion divisors do not supply linearly
   207	disjoint cyclic extensions.  This is the exact obstruction to the proposed
   208	`mu^2|d_1` shortcut.
   209	
   210	Equation (2.3) is still useful: it produces a second multiple zero divisor
   211	on `U`.  But a function having one multiple zero divisor need not have
   212	general fibre `A1`, and no theorem in the frozen package identifies `r` with
   213	a function of `t`.  Calling `r` a second ruling, or calling `E` a second bad
   214	fibre of `rho`, would add the missing conclusion by hand.
