   216	## 3. What the two polynomial sources actually see
   217	
   218	### 3.1 The original strict-block source
   219	
   220	The original first leg satisfies
   221	
   222	```text
   223	t o g_1-a=cP^mu.                                       (3.1)
   224	```
   225	
   226	Etaleness makes `g_1^*Phi` reduced.  Comparing divisors in (3.1) shows that
   227	`P` is squarefree and
   228	
   229	```text
   230	div(P)=g_1^*Phi.
   231	```
   232	
   233	The original composite `F` is Keller, so `c_0 o F` is also squarefree.  Its
   234	divisor contains `g_1^*Phi` once.  Thus there is a polynomial `S_F` with
   235	
   236	```text
   237	c_0 o F=P*S_F,             gcd(P,S_F)=1,               (3.2)
   238	r o g_1=c^(-1)S_F^mu.                                  (3.3)
   239	```
   240	
   241	This is the exact algebraic coupling furnished by the actual first
   242	polynomial.  The companion polynomial `q_F=b o F` shares no curve component
   243	with `P`, because `Phi` is not a component of `T=pi^{-1}(B) intersect U`.
   244	Their finite intersections record `T intersect Phi`; (3.1)--(3.3) impose no
   245	additional divisibility on their number.
