   291	### 3.3 The full `C_0`-pullback Euler ledger
   292	
   293	The exact `2/1/0` census also controls the Euler characteristic of
   294	
   295	```text
   296	D_0=pi^(-1)(C_0) intersect U=Supp(Phi+E).
   297	```
   298	
   299	Let `o` be the number of ordinary points of `C_0 intersect B`, and let
   300	`epsilon_c` be one or zero according as the charged cusp `c` lies on `C_0`.
   301	The omitted node `n` does not lie on `C_0`.  Fibre cardinalities of
   302	`D_0->C_0` are four off `B`, two at the `o` ordinary intersections, and one
   303	at `c` when `epsilon_c=1`.  Constructible Euler integration gives
   304	
   305	```text
   306	e(D_0)=4e(C_0)-2o-3epsilon_c.                           (3.8)
   307	```
   308	
   309	Let the singular points of `C_0` have `r_z>=2` normalization preimages, and
   310	put
   311	
   312	```text
   313	delta_top=sum_z(r_z-1),             s=#Sing(C_0).
   314	```
   315	
   316	The normalization is `A1`, so
   317	
   318	```text
   319	e(C_0)=1-delta_top.                                    (3.9)
   320	```
   321	
   322	Because the normalization is immersive, every singularity is a
   323	multibranch identification.  At each of its `r_z` preimages, the other local
   324	branches pull back into `E`; at a smooth point no residual branch meets
   325	`Phi`.  Hence
   326	
   327	```text
   328	#(Phi intersect E)=sum_z r_z=delta_top+s.
   329	```
   330	
   331	Using `e(Phi union E)=1+e(E)-#(Phi intersect E)` in (3.8) yields the exact
   332	residual ledger
   333	
   334	```text
   335	e(E)=3-3delta_top+s-2o-3epsilon_c.                      (3.10)
   336	```
   337	
   338	For one ordinary self-node away from `B` and no other intersection data,
   339	(3.10) gives `e(E)=1`; if that node lies at an ordinary point of `B`, it
   340	gives `e(E)=-1`.  Both are ordinary Euler characteristics of affine curves.
   341	Neither `Pic(U)=Z/mu` nor `K_U~0` fixes `e(E)`, so (3.10) is a sharp census
   342	identity rather than a contradiction.
