   344	## 4. Formanek makes `t` a primitive quartic element
   345	
   346	Put
   347	
   348	```text
   349	K=C(H_1,H_2),            M=C(U),            L=C(x,y).
   350	```
   351	
   352	The field tower has
   353	
   354	```text
   355	[M:K]=4,                 [L:M]=mu.                     (4.1)
   356	```
   357	
   358	Because `x` is a source coordinate, Formanek's theorem gives `L=K(x)`.
   359	Also `t-a=cx^mu`, so `x` has degree at most `mu` over `K(t)`.  Since
   360	`K(t) subset M subset L`, the tower law gives
   361	
   362	```text
   363	mu*[M:K(t)]=[L:K(t)]<=mu.
   364	```
   365	
   366	Therefore
   367	
   368	```text
   369	M=K(t),                  [L:K(t)]=mu.                  (4.2)
   370	```
   371	
   372	Let `P_t(Z) in K[Z]` be the monic minimal polynomial of `t`.  It has degree
   373	four.  Substitution gives a monic polynomial of degree `4mu` annihilating
   374	`x`.  Since `[L:K]=4mu`, it is the minimal polynomial:
   375	
   376	```text
   377	m_x(X)=c^(-4)P_t(a+cX^mu) is irreducible over K.        (4.3)
   378	```
   379	
   380	Thus the cyclic symmetry is encoded in an exact irreducible quartic
   381	composition.  This is equivalent, by the field tower, to irreducibility of
   382	`X^mu-(t-a)/c` over `M`; the fact that its norm below is a `mu`-fold divisor
   383	does not make the Kummer element a `p`-th power in `M`.
   384	
   385	Set `V=A2-B`.  The restriction `pi^{-1}(V)->V` is finite etale and is
   386	contained in `U`, so `t` is regular and integral there.  Since `O(V)` is
   387	normal, the coefficients of `P_t` lie in `O(V)`, and
   388	
   389	```text
   390	O(V)[t] isomorphic to O(V)[Z]/(P_t)                    (4.4)
   391	```
   392	
   393	is a free rank-four order whose normalization is the finite-etale algebra
   394	`O(pi^{-1}(V))`.
   395	
   396	There are two useful exact consequences.
   397	
   398	First, evaluate at the special ruling value.  A zero of `P_t(a)` on `V`
   399	means that one sheet has `t=a`, hence lies on `Phi`; its target lies on
   400	`C_0`.  Generically over `C_0`, there is one such sheet, the residue degree is
   401	one, and `t-a` has valuation `mu`.  Therefore
   402	
   403	```text
   404	div_V(P_t(a))=mu*(C_0 intersect V).                     (4.5)
   405	```
   406	
   407	Because the producer makes `B` irreducible and `C_0!=B`, if `b` and `c_0`
   408	are irreducible equations then for some `lambda in C*` and `k in Z`,
   409	
   410	```text
   411	P_t(a)=lambda*b^k*c_0^mu        in O(V)=C[u,v,b^(-1)]. (4.6)
   412	```
   413	
   414	At the generic point of `B`, the two companion valuations of `t-a` are zero
   415	because `C_0!=B`.  The ramification prime has residue degree one.  The norm
   416	valuation formula therefore identifies `k` with the valuation of `a-t` at
   417	that ramification prime (with a negative value recording a pole).  The
   418	partition `(2,1,1)` fixes its ramification index two but does not fix this
   419	valuation.  Neither the cusp nor node packet supplies the missing integer.
   420	
   421	The exponent `mu` in (4.6) is not bounded by the degree four in `Z`:
   422	coefficients of `P_t` may have arbitrarily high target degree and poles along
   423	`B`.  Thus (4.6) is a norm identity, not a degree contradiction.
   424	
   425	Second, compare discriminants.  At every height-one prime of `V`, the
   426	normalization algebra is etale and hence has unit discriminant.  The standard
   427	index-discriminant identity for the two rank-four lattices in (4.4) gives
   428	
   429	```text
   430	div_V(Disc(P_t))=2*I_t                                 (4.7)
   431	```
   432	
   433	for an effective index divisor `I_t`.  A collision of two `t`-values outside
   434	`B` makes `Disc(P_t)` vanish, so it lies on `I_t`.  This is nonnormality of
   435	the monogenic order, not ramification of its etale normalization.
   436	
   437	To see the local equation, pass to the completed strict henselization at an
   438	off-`B` collision.  The quartic etale algebra splits into four copies of a
   439	regular complete local ring.  For the two selected normalization branches of
   440	`C_0`, choose local equations `u=0` and `v=0`.  Divisibility by `mu*Phi`
   441	gives, after absorbing unit `mu`-th roots,
   442	
   443	```text
   444	t_1-a=u^mu,              t_2-a=v^mu.                   (4.8)
   445	```
   446	
   447	The two sheet values coincide along
   448	
   449	```text
   450	t_1-t_2=u^mu-v^mu=product_(zeta^mu=1)(u-zeta*v).       (4.9)
   451	```
   452	
   453	If the immersed branches are tangent, the factors in (4.9) need not define
   454	distinct reduced curves; no transversality is asserted.  In the transverse
   455	case they are `mu` distinct coincidence branches.  Along them the common
   456	value of `t` varies, so nearby ordinary `A1` fibres contain pairs with the
   457	same `pi`-image.  The fibres themselves remain ordinary affine lines.
   458	
   459	At a collision on an ordinary point of `B`, the two unramified companion
   460	sheets still give two regular functions satisfying (4.8)--(4.9).  The
   461	rank-two ramified factor may carry a pole of `t`, so (4.4) need not extend
   462	across `B`.  Treating `t` as integral on the ramification boundary is another
   463	invalid shortcut.
   464	
   465	## 5. Exact completed-local quartic controls
   466	
   467	The following controls prove that none of the preceding phenomena is a
   468	formal-local contradiction.  They do **not** assert that the local algebras
   469	glue to the charged global `S_4` cover or arise from a global Keller map.
   470	
   471	### 5.1 Collision away from `B`
   472	
   473	Let `R=C[[u,v]]` and
   474	
   475	```text
   476	S=R^4,
   477	tau=(u^mu,v^mu,1,2),
   478	P(Z)=(Z-u^mu)(Z-v^mu)(Z-1)(Z-2).                       (5.1)
   479	```
   480	
   481	Then `S` is a rank-four finite-etale `R`-algebra and is the normalization of
   482	the generically monogenic order `R[tau]`.  Near the origin,
   483	
   484	```text
   485	Disc(P)=unit*(u^mu-v^mu)^2.                             (5.2)
   486	```
   487	
   488	Take the local target curve `C_0` to have equation `uv=0`.  On the first
   489	sheet designate `u=0` as the local branch of `Phi`; on the second designate
   490	`v=0`.  The other branch on each sheet is residual.  Then
   491	
   492	```text
   493	(uv)^mu/u^mu=v^mu,       (uv)^mu/v^mu=u^mu,            (5.3)
   494	```
   495	
   496	which is exactly the inverse-Kummer identity on the selected sheets.  The
   497	normalization is etale and has trivial canonical module, while the
   498	monogenic order has precisely the square index divisor (5.2).  Thus a
   499	same-fibre node and a nonzero conductor coexist with quartic etaleness and
   500	`K=0`.
   501	
   502	For a global plane-curve control of the singularity type, the polynomial map
   503	
   504	```text
   505	s |-> (s^2, s(s^2-1))
   506	```
   507	
   508	is immersive and identifies `s=1` with `s=-1`.  Its image
   509	
   510	```text
   511	Y^2=X(X-1)^2
   512	```
   513	
   514	has an ordinary node at `(1,0)` and one point at infinity.  This only shows
   515	that an immersive polynomial normalization can have the required
   516	self-identification; it is not an asymptotic curve of a constructed Keller
   517	map.
   518	
   519	### 5.2 Collision at an ordinary `(2,1,1)` point
   520	
   521	Again let `R=C[[u,v]]`, put `w=u+v`, and take
   522	
   523	```text
   524	S=R[z]/(z^2-w)  times R times R.                        (5.4)
   525	```
   526	
   527	It is finite flat of rank four.  At `w=0` its geometric fibre has lengths
   528	`(2,1,1)`, and the first factor is the simple ramification factor.  The two
   529	`R` factors are the unramified companions.  On them set
   530	
   531	```text
   532	t_1-a=u^mu,              t_2-a=v^mu,                   (5.5)
   533	```
   534	
   535	and use `uv=0` for the two local branches of `C_0`.  On the ramified factor,
   536	`t-a=z^(-1)` is a legitimate local model on the etale open obtained by
   537	deleting `z=0`; it emphasizes that `t` need not extend over the boundary.
   538	Equations (5.3) and (5.5) then hold on the companion sheets, while the fibre
   539	partition is exactly `(2,1,1)`.  Both companions can therefore belong to the
   540	same special ruling fibre without changing the quartic packet.
   541	
   542	The companion equality locus is `u^mu-v^mu=0`.  For some parities and
   543	relative branch positions it can contain the local branch `B`; otherwise it
   544	merely crosses `B`.  Neither case changes the geometric fibre lengths.
   545	
   546	### 5.3 The charged cusp and node packets
   547	
   548	The standard cubic-discriminant algebra gives the cusp control.  For
   549	`R=C[[s,w]]`, let
   550	
   551	```text
   552	S_c=R[z]/(z^3-3s*z-2w)  times R.                        (5.6)
   553	```
   554	
   555	Its discriminant is a nonzero scalar times `s^3-w^2`.  At the cusp origin
   556	the fibre lengths are `(3,1)`; at a generic smooth point of the discriminant
   557	they are `(2,1,1)`.  The hypersurface factor in (5.6) is regular because the
   558	defining equation has nonzero derivative with respect to `w`.
   559	
   560	For the node control, with `R=C[[u,v]]`, take
   561	
   562	```text
   563	S_n=R[z]/(z^2-u)  times R[w]/(w^2-v).                   (5.7)
   564	```
   565	
   566	The discriminant support is `uv=0`.  The origin has fibre lengths `(2,2)`;
   567	geometrically, a generic point of either branch has `(2,1,1)`.  Both source
   568	factors are regular.
   569	
   570	Equations (5.4), (5.6), and (5.7) have exactly the mutually required local
   571	packet types: transposition generically, a three-cycle at the cusp, and two
   572	disjoint transpositions at the node.  The extra singularity belongs
   573	to `C_0`, not to `B`, and hence does not add a cusp or node to the charged
   574	census of `B`.  What remains unproved is global gluing with all infinity and
   575	Keller constraints; local algebra alone cannot supply the exclusion.
   576	
   577	## 6. Why `K_U~0` and adjunction do not close the horn
   578	
   579	Restrict the divisor relation (2.2) to `Phi`.  Since `Phi=A1`,
   580	
   581	```text
   582	Pic(Phi)=0,
   583	O_U(Phi)|Phi isomorphic to O_Phi,
   584	O_U(E)|Phi isomorphic to O_Phi.                         (6.1)
   585	```
   586	
   587	Adjunction gives the same result:
   588	
   589	```text
   590	omega_Phi isomorphic to (omega_U tensor O_U(Phi))|Phi
   591	             isomorphic to O_Phi.                      (6.2)
   592	```
   593	
   594	The section cutting out `E intersect Phi` is therefore a polynomial section
   595	of a trivial line bundle on `A1`.  Such a section may have any finite
   596	effective zero divisor; its balancing pole lies at the missing point at
   597	infinity.  In particular, the at-least-two collision points do not conflict
   598	with (6.1)--(6.2).  A projective intersection number cannot be imported
   599	without the boundary terms of a chosen completion.
   600	
   601	There is an analogous canonical firewall for the primitive quartic order.
   602	The scheme
   603	
   604	```text
   605	X=Spec(O(V)[Z]/(P_t)) subset V times A1
   606	```
   607	
   608	is a hypersurface and hence Gorenstein.  Its normalization is finite etale
   609	over `V`, so both sides have locally trivial dualizing modules.  Finite
   610	duality identifies the relative dual fractional ideal with the normalized
   611	dualizing module; after the displayed trivializations that fractional ideal
   612	is principal.  This does not say that the order is normal or that its
   613	conductor is the unit ideal.
   614	The split model (5.1)--(5.3) realizes a nonempty square index divisor while
   615	both normalized sheets have trivial canonical module.
   616	
   617	Finally, `T=div_U(b o pi)` is principal and has trivial dualizing sheaf by
   618	adjunction, exactly as in the producer.  Neither (2.4) nor (6.2) introduces
   619	`b`, so `K_U~0` supplies no new congruence for
   620	`N=#(T intersect Phi)` and does not select either row of (3.7).
   621	
   622	## 7. Maximum-safe conclusion and next gate
   623	
   624	Promote, conditional on the provisional producer, exactly the following.
   625	
   626	> **Inverse-Kummer and primitive-quartic control.**  The residual divisor in
   627	> `pi^*C_0=Phi+E` has class `-[Phi]`, and the regular function
   628	> `r=(c_0 o pi)^mu/(t-a)` has divisor `mu E`.  Its Kummer extension is the
   629	> original cyclic extension, not an independent one.  On the two polynomial
   630	> sources this is the exact factorization (0.3)--(0.4).  Formanek's theorem
   631	> makes `t` a primitive generator of `C(U)/C(A2)`.  The same-fibre collision
   632	> therefore forces a monogenic index/coincidence divisor, locally
   633	> `u^mu-v^mu=0`, but not ramification of `pi` or a bad fibre of `rho`.
   634	> Trivial canonical class and the local quartic `(2,1,1)`, `(3,1)`, and
   635	> `(2,2)` algebras are compatible with this configuration.
   636	
   637	Do not promote any of the following:
   638	
   639	1. `[E]` is independent of `[Phi]`, or `mu^2|d_1`;
   640	2. `E` is irreducible, `r` is an `A1`-fibration, or `E` is another fibre of
   641	   `rho`;
   642	3. the self-identification is transverse, or the roots in (3.6) are simple;
   643	4. the monogenic index divisor is a branch/nonproper divisor of `pi` or `H`;
   644	5. `t` extends regularly over the ramification boundary `R`;
   645	6. the completed-local controls glue to a global cover or a Keller map;
   646	7. `K_U~0` makes `E intersect Phi` empty or fixes its degree.
   647	
   648	The cheapest genuine closing moves are now sharply separated.
   649	
   650	1. **Index-at-infinity gate.**  Prove that the forced coincidence divisor of
   651	   the primitive quartic order contributes a positive Orevkov--Chau boundary
   652	   correction.  Since the producer's budget is saturated, one positive unit
   653	   would contradict the horn.  The missing theorem must distinguish index
   654	   of `O(V)[t]` from ramification of its etale normalization.
   655	2. **Second-fibration gate.**  Use the global pseudo-plane geometry to prove
   656	   that a regular function with `div(r)=mu E`, where `[E]=-[Phi]` and
   657	   `E intersect Phi` contains the collision points, has general fibre `A1`.
   658	   Only then could uniqueness of the `A1`-ruling force a relation with `t`.
   659	3. **Companion-resultant gate.**  Combine the norm identity (4.6) with the
   660	   actual polynomial `q_H=b o H` to force the index divisor to meet `B` at
   661	   the cusp or node, rather than at an allowed ordinary point or only at
   662	   infinity.  The existing Euler identity alone does not do this.
   663	
   664	For the charged strict-block horn, `d_1>=2` and the producer conditionally
   665	gives `mu=d_1` after minimal-degree replacement.  The `d_1=1` pseudo-plane
   666	row is empty already because `mu>=2` and `mu|d_1`.  This says nothing about
   667	an arbitrary canonical rank-four normalization outside the charged
   668	pseudo-plane package; no `d_1=1` specialization is made here.
   669	
   670	No replay is attached.  The new statements are divisor identities, field
   671	tower arithmetic, and exact completed-local algebra formulas valid for all
   672	`mu`; sampling finitely many values would not verify the theorem-level
   673	steps.
