   141	## 2. What Orevkov--Chau actually counts
   142	
   143	Orevkov starts with a smooth compactification of the polynomial source and
   144	regularizes the polynomial map by blowups at infinity.  Write `L_F` for the
   145	irreducible boundary components on which the regular extension is
   146	nonconstant.  Constant boundary trees and the polar boundary are collapsed,
   147	producing a continuous constant-multiplicity map
   148	
   149	```text
   150	H*:X*->one-point compactification of A2.
   151	```
   152	
   153	Orevkov defines the multiplicity at a source point `x` as the largest `k`
   154	such that every neighborhood of `x` contains `k` points with one common
   155	image.  For a local biholomorphism it is one, even if a distant second
   156	source point has the same image.  For each `l subset L_F`, let `mu_l` be the
   157	generic local multiplicity on its affine image after the collapse.  Lemma
   158	4.2 is exactly
   159	
   160	```text
   161	N-1=sum_(l subset L_F)
   162	       [mu_l+sum_(x in l-{infinity})(mu_x-mu_l)],       (2.1)
   163	```
   164	
   165	where `N=deg_geo(H)`.  Semicontinuity makes every correction nonnegative.
   166	
   167	Chau Remark 4.9 restates (2.1) as formula (4.9).  Its formula (4.10) rewrites
   168	the same terms in Newton--Puiseux charts: the positive corrections occur
   169	where the local degree of a boundary chart exceeds its generic value.
   170	Chau Lemma 4.3 identifies those events with critical/singular
   171	parametrization values.  A node obtained by identifying two distinct
   172	immersed parameter values is not such an event at either parameter.
   173	
   174	Three consequences are important here.
   175	
   176	1. The outer sum is over actual nonconstant **source boundary components**,
   177	   not target curves and not divisors of an auxiliary discriminant.
   178	2. Two boundary components with the same image are both charged their base
   179	   terms; there is neither cancellation nor an extra term merely because
   180	   their images agree.
   181	3. Two distinct points of one immersed boundary line with the same image
   182	   still have `mu_x=1` when the ambient extension is etale at both.  The
   183	   self-identification is budget-free beyond the line's already charged base
   184	   term.
   185	
   186	For the deleted cyclic lines, conclusion 3 gives precisely the `mu-1` in
   187	(1.2).  The index collision cannot be charged a second time without proving
   188	that it creates a new boundary component or a local-degree jump.
   189	
   190	## 3. Index discriminant versus normalized ramification
   191	
   192	Let
   193	
   194	```text
   195	A=O(V),             V=A2-B,
   196	S=O(pi^(-1)(V)),    K=Frac(A),
   197	M=Frac(S)=K(t),     O=A[t]=A[Z]/(P_t).                  (3.1)
   198	```
   199	
   200	The algebra `S` is finite etale of rank four over `A`; `O` is a free
   201	rank-four order with normalization `S`.
   202	
   203	Localize at a height-one prime `q` of `A`.  The DVR lattice `S_q/O_q` has
   204	Smith exponents `a_1,...,a_4>=0`.  If `C` is the change-of-basis matrix from
   205	an `A_q`-basis of `S_q` to the power basis of `O_q`, then
   206	
   207	```text
   208	v_q(det C)=sum_i a_i=length_(A_q)(S_q/O_q).
   209	```
   210	
   211	The trace Gram matrices satisfy
   212	
   213	```text
   214	Gram(O_q)=C^T Gram(S_q) C.
   215	```
   216	
   217	Taking determinants gives the exact discriminant-index formula
   218	
   219	```text
   220	v_q(Disc(P_t))
   221	 =v_q(Disc(S_q/A_q))+2 length_(A_q)(S_q/O_q)
   222	 =2 length_(A_q)(S_q/O_q).                              (3.2)
   223	```
   224	
   225	The last equality is etaleness.  In differential language,
   226	`Omega_(S/A)=0` and the different of the normalization is the unit ideal.
   227	The nonunit discriminant in (3.2) belongs only to the nonnormal suborder
   228	`O`.
   229	
   230	Over a strict henselization, write the four values of `t` as
   231	`t_1,...,t_4`.  Then
   232	
   233	```text
   234	Disc(P_t)=product_(i<j)(t_i-t_j)^2.                     (3.3)
   235	```
   236	
   237	At the same-fibre collision, two selected values have
   238	
   239	```text
   240	t_1-a=u^mu,              t_2-a=v^mu,
   241	t_1-t_2=u^mu-v^mu.                                      (3.4)
   242	```
   243	
   244	This is an off-diagonal equality of two etale sections.  Ramification would
   245	instead be failure of one section to be etale along the diagonal.  Equations
   246	(3.2)--(3.4) make the distinction exact.
