   154	is regular.
   155	
   156	For every prime divisor `E` on `U`, surjectivity of `f_mu` gives a prime
   157	divisor `E'` on `X` above it.  Since `f_mu` is etale, its ramification index
   158	at these generic points is one, and
   159	
   160	```text
   161	ord_E(f)=ord_(E')(f_mu^*f)>=0.
   162	```
   163	
   164	A rational function on a normal affine variety is regular exactly when all
   165	of its codimension-one orders are nonnegative.  Hence `f in O(U)`, proving
   166	
   167	```text
   168	C[x,y] intersect M=O(U).                              (2.1)
   169	```
   170	
   171	This also follows from faithfully flat descent, but the valuation proof
   172	makes the later boundary statement transparent.
   173	
   174	## 3. Only one valuation controls polynomiality
   175	
   176	Remove the multiple fibre.  The full cyclic cover restricts to
   177	
   178	```text
   179	D -> U-Phi,
   180	```
   181	
   182	a finite-etale Galois cover with group `C_mu`.  Since the Laurent change
   183	`Y=y+g(x)` is regular on `D`,
   184	
   185	```text
   186	O(D)=C[x,x^(-1),Y].
   187	```
   188	
   189	The diagonal action in (0.1) has invariant ring
   190	
   191	```text
   192	O(D)^(C_mu)=C[X,X^(-1),S],
   193	X=x^mu,                         S=xY.                 (3.1)
   194	```
   195	
   196	Indeed every invariant monomial `x^aY^b` has `a-b=0 mod mu` and, because
   197	`x` is invertible, is a Laurent power of `X` times a power of `S`.
   198	Therefore
   199	
   200	```text
   201	O(U-Phi)=C[X,X^(-1),S].                               (3.2)
   202	```
   203	
   204	The complement of `U-Phi` in the smooth normal surface `U` is the single
   205	prime divisor `Phi`.  The Krull-domain intersection theorem gives
   206	
   207	```text
   208	O(U)=O(U-Phi) intersect O_(U,eta_Phi)
   209	    =C[X,X^(-1),S] intersect O_(U,eta_Phi)            (3.3)
   210	```
   211	
   212	inside `M`.  Write `nu=ord_Phi`.  The etale line `L_0->Phi` has degree one,
   213	so `nu` is computed after pullback by the `x`-adic order at the generic
   214	point of `L_0`.  Since
   215	
   216	```text
   217	X=x^mu,                  S=xy+xg(x)
   218	```
   219	
   220	and `g` has leading term `c_r x^(-r)`, `c_r!=0`, one obtains
   221	
   222	```text
   223	nu(X)=mu,                nu(S)=1-r.                   (3.4)
   224	```
   225	
   226	Concretely, every element of (3.2) is a finite Laurent polynomial
   227	`F(X,S)`.  Substitute `X=x^mu` and `S=xy+xg(x)` and collect powers of `x`.
   228	Membership in (3.3) is exactly cancellation of all negative powers.  The
   229	coefficients retain the residue parameter `y`, so cancellations, rather
   230	than a naive scalar weight inequality, matter.
   231	
   232	This is the degree-independent polynomiality classification.  It contains
   233	no reference to the quartic subfield `K`.
   234	
   235	## 4. The logarithmic Jacobian equation
   236	
   237	The Laurent translation has determinant one, and
   238	
   239	```text
   240	dX wedge dS
   241	 =d(x^mu) wedge d(xY)
   242	 =mu x^mu dx wedge dy
   243	 =mu X dx wedge dy.                                  (4.1)
   244	```
   245	
   246	Write `H_i=F_i(X,S)` using `H_i in M`.  The chain rule and the constant
   247	Jacobian `J_(x,y)(H_1,H_2)=c` yield
   248	
   249	```text
   250	J_(X,S)(F_1,F_2)=c/(mu X).                            (4.2)
   251	```
   252	
   253	On `U-Phi`, the right side is a unit.  Across `Phi`, the pair `(X,S)` is not
   254	a regular coordinate system: `X` vanishes to order `mu` and `S` has a pole.
   255	Consequently the pole in (4.2) is not ramification of `pi`.  It is the
   256	Laurent expression of the regular nowhere-zero form
   257	
   258	```text
   259	dx wedge dy=(dX wedge dS)/(mu X).                     (4.3)
   260	```
   261	
   262	This is precisely compatible with `K_U=0`.
   263	
   264	The field condition is separate:
   265	
   266	```text
   267	K=C(F_1,F_2),                 [M:K]=4,
   268	M=K(X).                                              (4.4)
   269	```
   270	
   271	Equations (3.3), (4.2), and (4.4), together with the boundary packet, are
   272	the exact quartic algebraic gate.  Dropping any one of them loses essential
   273	information.
   274	
   275	## 5. Explicit intersection in the primitive single-pole model
   276	
   277	This section gives a sharp global control on (3.3).  Fix `r>=2`, put
   278	`d=r-1`, assume `gcd(d,mu)=1`, and take
   279	
   280	```text
   281	g(x)=x^(-r),             Y=y+x^(-r),
   282	w=x^rY=1+x^r y,
   283	z=(w^mu-1)/x^r
   284	 =y(1+w+...+w^(mu-1)).                                (5.1)
   285	```
   286	
   287	Both `w` and `z` are polynomials in the retained `(x,y)` chart.  Let
   288	
   289	```text
   290	B=C[x,w,z]/(x^r z-w^mu+1).                            (5.2)
   291	```
   292	
   293	The diagonal action
   294	
   295	```text
   296	gamma(x,w,z)=(zeta x,zeta^d w,zeta^(-r)z)             (5.3)
   297	```
   298	
   299	preserves (5.2).  It is free: away from `x=0` the first coordinate has no
   300	nontrivial stabilizer, while at `x=0` one has `w^mu=1`, and multiplication
   301	by `zeta^d` is free on those roots because `gcd(d,mu)=1`.
   302	
   303	The zero fibre consists of `mu` disjoint lines `x=0,w=alpha`.  The action
   304	permutes them transitively.  Deleting all but `w=1` gives the retained `A2`
   305	chart (5.1), and these translated charts cover `Spec B`.
   306	
   307	If `f in C[x,y] intersect M`, invariance makes `f` regular on every
   308	translated chart, hence on `Spec B`; conversely an invariant element of `B`
   309	is polynomial on the retained chart.  Therefore
   310	
   311	```text
   312	C[x,y] intersect M=B^(C_mu).                          (5.4)
   313	```
   314	
   315	Because (5.3) is diagonal and its defining relation is invariant, (5.4) is
   316	spanned as a `C`-vector space, and generated as an algebra, by the residue
   317	classes of monomials
   318	
   319	```text
   320	x^a w^b z^c,
   321	a,b,c>=0,                 a+d b-r c=0 mod mu.         (5.5)
   322	```
   323	
   324	Their retained-chart pullbacks are the explicit polynomials
   325	
   326	```text
   327	x^a(1+x^r y)^b
   328	 y^c(1+w+...+w^(mu-1))^c,
   329	w=1+x^r y.                                          (5.6)
   330	```
   331	
   332	Equations (5.5)--(5.6) are a finite Hilbert-basis description suitable for
   333	bounded computation.  They also show why simply comparing the two weights
   334	`nu(X),nu(S)` misses cancellations.
   335	
   336	## 6. Polynomiality permits arbitrary line functions and an etale first jet
   337	
   338	On the retained line `x=0`, formulas (5.1) give
   339	
   340	```text
   341	w=1,                         z=mu y.                  (6.1)
   342	```
   343	
   344	For any `c>=0`, choose `b>=0` with
   345	
   346	```text
   347	d b-r c=0 mod mu;
   348	```
   349	
   350	this is possible because `d` is invertible modulo `mu`.  The invariant
   351	monomial `w^b z^c` restricts to `(mu y)^c`.  Hence
   352	
   353	```text
   354	B^(C_mu) -> C[y]
   355	```
   356	
   357	is surjective.  In particular, polynomiality imposes no degree or
   358	injectivity restriction on the normalization parametrization of `C_0`.
   359	
   360	There is also no first-order obstruction.  Choose `b,c` modulo `mu` so that
   361	
   362	```text
   363	1+d b=0 mod mu,
   364	d c-r=0 mod mu.                                      (6.2)
   365	```
   366	
   367	Then
   368	
   369	```text
   370	u=x w^b,                    v=w^c z                  (6.3)
   371	```
   372	
   373	are invariant polynomials.  Along the entire retained line,
   374	
   375	```text
   376	u=x+O(x^(r+1)),             v=mu y+O(x^r),
   377	J_(x,y)(u,v)|_(x=0)=mu.                                (6.4)
   378	```
   379	
   380	Thus the quotient already has explicit functions which are etale to first
   381	order along `Phi` and give a coordinate on it.
   382	
   383	They do not form a global Keller pair.  A direct calculation with the form
   384	`dx wedge dw/x^r` gives
   385	
   386	```text
   387	{u,v}
   388	 =w^(b+c-1)
   389	   [(c+b r+mu)w^mu-(c+b r)],                          (6.5)
   390	```
   391	
   392	which specializes to `mu` at `w=1` but is nonconstant.  Equation (6.5) is
   393	a useful sharp control: all polynomiality and local-etaleness conditions can
   394	hold while the global constant-Jacobian equation remains the only failure.
   395	
   396	## 7. What degree four does and does not force locally
   397	
   398	Let `eta` be the generic point of `C_0`.  Since `pi|Phi` is the immersive
   399	normalization of degree one and `pi` is etale at the generic point of
   400	`Phi`, the valuation `nu` restricts to the `C_0`-adic valuation of `K` with
   401	
   402	```text
   403	e(nu/K)=1,                       f(nu/K)=1.             (7.1)
   404	```
   405	
   406	The other three sheets of the quartic extension can be etale as well.  This
   407	is not merely a dimension count.  Put `k=C(y)`, `A_0=k[[u]]`, and consider
   408	the rank-four etale algebra
   409	
   410	```text
   411	E=A_0 times A_0 times A_0 times A_0.
   412	```
   413	
   414	The element
   415	
   416	```text
   417	T=(u^mu,1,2,3)                                       (7.2)
   418	```
   419	
   420	is primitive, with polynomial
   421	
   422	```text
   423	(Z-u^mu)(Z-1)(Z-2)(Z-3).                             (7.3)
   424	```
   425	
   426	Its discriminant is a unit: the four residues `0,1,2,3` are distinct.  On
   427	the first sheet `ord(T)=mu`, exactly as `nu(X)=mu`, while all four branches
   428	are unramified and one has residue degree one.  A global quartic field can
   429	have precisely this split completed algebra at the place.
   430	

